---
<Base title="Architecture">
  <h1>Architecture</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    How the project is put together today, why each part is there, and how I would build the same system for
    production. The first diagram is what actually runs. The second is a design only and is labelled that way.
  </p>

  <h2>What runs today</h2>
  <Diagram {...today} />

  <h3>Why it is shaped this way</h3>
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Reason</th><th>Trade-off</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Medallion layers (bronze, silver, gold)</td><td>Raw data is kept, so a cleaning bug can be fixed and replayed without downloading again</td><td>More tables and more processing to maintain</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Quarantine instead of delete</td><td>Every rejected row is kept with the rules it failed, so totals reconcile to the row</td><td>A second table to store and explain</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Rules as data (SQL strings)</td><td>One list drives the notebook, the pipeline, the quality report and the tests</td><td>Rules have to be valid in Spark SQL</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Two implementations of the rules</td><td>The notebook and the declarative pipeline cross-check each other</td><td>Two places to change; a test catches any drift</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Unity Catalog as the documentation source</td><td>Descriptions live with the tables; the site reads them back, so they cannot drift</td><td>Comments must be applied by a notebook run</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Additive cubes, averages computed in the browser</td><td>Any filter combination gives an exact average from sums and counts</td><td>Only pre-chosen dimensions can be filtered</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Static site, no server</td><td>Free to host, fast, keeps working when the Databricks workspace is idle</td><td>Data updates need an export and a commit</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h3>Limits of the current setup</h3>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The hand-off is manual.</strong> Exported files are downloaded and committed by hand. Free Edition is meant for
      learning, so the public site does not depend on the workspace being reachable.</li>
    <li><strong>Loads are full, not incremental.</strong> Each run reprocesses the whole year. That is fine at about 41
      million rows but would not be at ten years.</li>
    <li><strong>Runs are started by hand.</strong> Nothing is scheduled yet.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Production design <span class="tag">designed, not built</span></h2>
  <p class="lede">
    The same data flow, built to run unattended and to grow. None of this is implemented in the project; it shows how
    each limit above would be removed.
  </p>
  <Diagram {...prod} />
  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Today</th><th>Production design</th><th>What it fixes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Notebook downloads all 12 files</td><td>Auto Loader on a landing folder</td><td>Only new files are processed, exactly once</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Full rebuild each run</td><td>Incremental declarative pipeline</td><td>Cost and run time grow with new data, not total data</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Notebooks started by hand</td><td>Monthly Lakeflow Job with alerts</td><td>Runs unattended; a bad month is flagged, not published</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Code pulled into the workspace by hand</td><td>Asset Bundles deployed by CI after tests pass</td><td>Dev and prod stay identical and reviewed</td></tr>
        <tr><td>One schema in one catalog</td><td>Separate dev and prod catalogs with permissions</td><td>Experiments cannot touch published data</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Manual download and commit</td><td>Export task + site deploy hook</td><td>The site updates itself after a good run</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="lede small">
    Related pages: <a href="/advanced/">Advanced features</a> (pipeline, catalog and benchmark),
    <a href="/case-studies/">Case studies</a> (problems met while building this).
  </p>
</Base>

<style>
  h3 { margin:1.5rem 0 .4rem; font-size:1.05rem; }
  .card { overflow-x:auto; }
  .small { font-size:.85rem; }
  .tag { font-size:.7rem; font-weight:600; vertical-align:middle; padding:.15rem .5rem; border:1px solid var(--accent);
         color:var(--accent); border-radius:999px; margin-left:.4rem; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:.05em; }
</style>
